# Naming convention

The names below are rules and part of the public API (invariant 14). The full inventory of exports, options, flags and reasons is on the pages in the table at the end; read the owning page before adding or renaming a public identifier, option, flag, reason or wire field.

- The brand is the noun. Type `PermDock`, instance `permdock`. `create*` sets up once at module scope, `get*` resolves per request on the server, `use*` reads on the client. Core and `permdock/pdp` build an instance with `getPermDock(policy, user)` (async) and `getPermDockSync` (throws when an input answers with a Promise); they export no `createPermDock`. Every factory that hands out instances is `createPermDock`: every server and agent adapter and the four feature entries `permdock/agent`, `permdock/workflow`, `permdock/delivery` and `permdock/chat`. Never a function that returns `X | Promise<X>`. Its return type is `<Entry>PermDock<V>` (`ServerPermDock`, `AgentPermDock`, `WorkflowPermDock`, `DeliveryPermDock`, `ChatPermDock`), and the import path names the entry (`permdock/next`, `permdock/hono`). A factory that returns a handler or a store instead of an instance is named after what it returns, without `create` (`evaluationsHandler`, `nativeStore`). Never `NextDock`, `HonoDock`, `dock`, `ability`, `can` as a definer, `create*Kernel`, or `$`-prefixed members.
- Instance access: `getPermDock(input)` is the instance getter on every factory result (`permdock/server`, `node`, `hono`, `express`, `fastify`, `elysia`, `nest`, `trpc`, `orpc`, `terminal`, `openai`, `claude-agent`, `eve`, and `WorkflowPermDock.getPermDock(ticket)`). Middleware is `middleware()` (`hono`, `express`, `elysia`, `trpc`, `orpc`), and a fastify plugin value is a function too. The mounted endpoint is `permdockHandler()`, always of the shape `() => <framework handler>`. Never a `permdock()` member.
- Methods and feature functions: the decisions every app makes are methods on the instance: `decideEach(permission, rows)` and `decideFor(principalId, permission, row)`; `decideFor` never widens the caller and never consumes a `limit`; `permittedIds` and `mayUse` are methods too. A feature's own request is a free function that takes the instance first, so a bundler drops the feature when an app does not import it: `decideCredential(permdock, request)`, `decideAutomation(permdock, request)`, `decideSupportSession(permdock, request)`, `decideRoleChange(permdock, change)`, `activate(permdock, input)`, `whoCan(permdock, permission, row)`, `simulateArazzo(permdock, plan)`. They read the instance's internals and deny with `unsupported` (or list nothing) on an instance built from a snapshot.
- Generics follow the instance: a factory that hands out an instance takes `Policy<TUser, TPrincipal, V>` and types it `PermDock<V>` everywhere it reaches user code (the `<Entry>PermDock<V>` return type, request and context types such as `ExpressPermDockRequest<…, V>`, `ConvexPermDockContext<TCtx, V>`, middleware results, `PdpPermDock<V>`). An adapter whose framework cannot carry a type into the handler offers a typed `withPermDock(handler)` wrapper instead of augmenting the framework's types. Each such adapter has a `tests/<adapter>/vocabulary.test-d.ts` over `tests/fixtures/vocabulary.ts`. Adapters that hand out no instance (`ai-sdk`, `mcp`, `a2a`, `authzen`, `openapi`, `ssf`) and those whose framework erases it (`nest` parameter decorators, `trpc`'s `AnyTRPCMiddlewareFunction`) take `V` only through the policy argument.
- UI: `permdock/react` exports `PermDockProvider`, `usePermDock`, `usePermission`, `<Protected>` and the `use*` hooks; servers use the async `getPermDock` / `getPermission` / `requirePermission(permission, data?, options?)`, where `requirePermission` turns a denial into `forbidden()` / `unauthorized()`. `protectAction(permission, { input, data?, tenant? })(handler)` wraps a Server Action and resolves to an `ActionResult` (`{ ok: true, value }` or `{ ok: false, problem }`); the second action argument is `{ approval }`, never `token`. Vue uses `use*` composables, Svelte `setPermDock` / `getPermDock` plus readable stores, Solid accessors. `permdock/react-native` takes a required `subjectId` and names helpers by what they wrap or do: `secureStoreStorage`, `mmkvStorage`, `appStateForeground`, `netInfoOnline`, `usePermissionGuard`, `useSnapshotReady`, `powerSyncSource`. Never a CASL-style `<Can>`; no components beyond `<Protected>` and the `<PermissionBoundary>` error boundary in `permdock/next/client`, `permdock/vue`, `permdock/svelte` and `permdock/solid` (with `usePermissionBoundary` outside Svelte, where the snippet receives the state). The boundary renders a fallback for a thrown PermDock error and never gates; its props are `denied` and `approval`, the same names as `<Protected>`'s slots (`pending`, `denied`, `approval`) and the provider `defaults`. Never `fallback` for a denied slot: in React, Vue and Solid it means a loading or error state. `PermDockDeniedError` and `PermDockApprovalRequiredError` carry a stable `digest` (`PERMDOCK_DENIED;<permission>`, `PERMDOCK_APPROVAL_REQUIRED;<permission>;<token>`), read with `parsePermDockDigest`.
- Subjects: every provider is `subjectFrom<Source>(input, options)`, returns `Subject<<Source>Principal>` and never throws (an unverifiable input is the anonymous subject); each exports its `<Source>Principal` type (`ApiKeyPrincipal`, `CapabilityPrincipal`, `IntrospectionPrincipal`) and takes a `schema` option. The input comes first and options second: `subjectFromApiKey(key, options)`, `subjectFromBetterAuth(session, { auth, … })`, `subjectFromEve` and `actorFromEve`. Subject builders inside the package are not exported, and an SSF subject lookup is an `SsfSubjectResolver`. Types come from `PrincipalOf` / `SubjectOf`; never `declare module` augmentation or a `$Infer` accessor. Where a provider reads a value from verified claims is its `claims` option, a `ClaimsMap` (`id`, `roles`, `groups`, `entitlements`, `plans`, `tenant`, `memberships`, `kind`, `assurance`, `session`; each a dot path or a function), never a top-level `roles` / `tenant` path or a provider-specific name such as `globalRoles`. The SQL side names one claim per setting (`rls.tenantClaim`, `rls.roleClaim`, `supabaseClaims({ tenantClaim })`), because the database reads a top-level claim by name.
- Principals: `PrincipalSource` is the one identity lookup, passed as `principals`: `principal(id, { tenant? })` and `principalByIdentity({ kind: 'email' | 'chat', provider?, value, workspace? })` return a live `Principal` or `null` (`memoryPrincipalSource`, `testPrincipalSource`). It replaces every `owner(id)` callback; never a `ChatIdentitySource`. A `kind: 'contact'` principal is an external person (a customer contact, a portal user) with only `{ on }` memberships; it never approves, publishes or creates keys, and is read-only by default.
- Operations: `defineOperations(permissions, { name: { permission, data?, results?, meta? } })` returns a frozen `OperationMap`, with `meta` `{ risk?, effects?, title? }`. Agent tools, workflow nodes, MCP operations, `protect(operation)`, oRPC's `operationsRouter(operations, routes, { meta? })` and chat commands read the same map, and the catalog lists it as `operations`.
- Run tickets: a `RunTicket` (v1) is signed by `signRunTicket` through any `TokenSigner` as `vnd.permdock.run+jwt` and resolved by `subjectFromRunTicket(ticket, { verifier, principals, automations? })`. Its `kind` is `automation`, `job` or `caller`.
- Scopes: `definePolicy({ scopes })` declares named scopes in order, each `{ key, within? }`, one tree rooted at the first; `tenant` / `team` alias the first two. A `Membership` is `{ scope, id, within }` or `{ on }`; a role applies only at its own scope (no cascade), and RLS helpers are `permitted_<scope>_ids(p_grant)` plus the membership-only `member_<scope>_ids()`, and on Supabase `member_<scope>_ids_for(p_user)` (executable by `supabase_auth_admin` only, for `supabase.hook.claims` functions), `member_<scope>_ids_of(p_user)` for a scope named `team` or `teams` (executable by `authenticated`, no rows unless `p_user` is the caller, for better-supabase's `teamIdsOf`) and `permdock_user_id()`, the one subject read of every generated helper and policy (`auth.uid()`, null for an empty `sub`); in `database` mode `permdock_has_for(p_user, p_grant)` and `permitted_<scope>_ids_for(p_user, p_grant)` answer for a named user and no client role may execute them, and `permitted_<scope>_users(p_permission, p_id)` lists who holds a permission in one instance, also executable by no client role; with `rls.readOnlyActors`, `permdock_actor_may_write()` is what hand-written write policies call; `permdock_has_permission(p_permission)`, `permitted_<scope>_ids_by_permission(p_permission)` and `grant_keys(p_permission, p_scope, p_effect)` take a permission key, never a `#n` grant key; `permdock_role_permissions(p_role, p_scope, p_tenant, p_scope_id)` and `permdock_permission_keys()` (or `(p_scope)` for the keys roles hold on one scope) read a role's keys and the catalog for role editors, executable by `authenticated`, and `permdock_trusted_role_permissions` reads them for server code, executable by no client role and by the `rls.trustedReaders` roles; `permitted_<scope>_permission_keys(p_id[, p_keys])` lists the keys held on one instance in one set-based query (`_for(p_user, p_id[, p_keys])` in `database` mode). Never a hard-coded tenant/team kind in new code.
- Links: a share link is a `Capability` (v1) signed as `vnd.permdock.capability+jwt` by `signCapability` and resolved by `subjectFromCapability` into a `kind: 'link'` principal with one `{ on, roles, via: 'link' }` membership; it never holds a scope or global role. A link stored as a better-supabase share-link row resolves through `subjectFromShareLink(token, { shareLinks, links })` into the same `Subject<CapabilityPrincipal>`, unsigned, with no second principal type. `holder: 'key'` stays reserved. RLS reaches links only through `exchangeCapability` (a short-lived `anon` token, never `service_role`, no `sub`) and `permdock_capability_ids` under `--capabilities` ([link capabilities](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/capabilities.mdx)).
- Signed outputs: `typ` is `vnd.permdock.<format>+jwt` (RFC 6838 vendor tree), one of `snapshot`, `approval`, `policy`, `capability`, `run`, `support-session`, `evidence`; never `permdock-*+jwt`. The Cloud approver token keeps the spec's RFC 9068 `at+jwt`. `iss`, `aud`, `iat`, `exp` and `jti` are the only times and ids, the actor is `act`, delegation is `scope` plus `authorization_details`, and private claims are snake_case. Digests are `sha-256:<base64url>` over RFC 8785 JSON.
- Events and webhooks: a CloudEvents `type` is `com.permdock.<family>` or `com.permdock.<family>.<operation>` (`com.permdock.decision`, `com.permdock.approval.resolved`, `com.permdock.membership.added`), never `dev.permdock.*`. Outbound webhooks follow Standard Webhooks (`webhook-id`, `webhook-timestamp`, `webhook-signature`), signed by `webhookSigner` and checked by `webhookVerifier`; a JWS is only an offline evidence export. The SCIM group extension is `urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:extension:permdock:2.0:Group`, never a `urn:permdock:*` URN.
- Keys: an API key is an opaque `pdk_<id>_<secret>` whose SHA-256 hash the app stores next to a `Credential` (v1); `subjectFromApiKey(key, options)` in `permdock/server` is the only resolver and returns `Subject<ApiKeyPrincipal>`. A `user` key is its owner's live rights narrowed through `delegation` (scopes, RFC 9396 entries with `identifier`), and an optional `tenant` holds it to the owner's memberships there; a `service` key is a `kind: 'service'` principal with one `{ tenant, roles, via: 'credential' }` membership, created by `permdock.decideCredential` only within the creator's `assignableRoles` / `assignablePermissions` (`exceeds-creator`). A `feed` key is `pdfeed_<id>_<secret><checksum>`, all lowercase, bound to one resource (`on`, `roles`) and rotated instead of expiring; it is a `kind: 'feed'` principal and never a scope role, an approver or a creator of keys. Tenant rules come from a `SettingsSource` (`credentials: { maxTtl, kinds, approval, allowNoExpiry }`); a user or service key without expiry needs `allowNoExpiry`, and a feed does not. `subjectFromApiKey` takes `kinds` to accept feeds only. The key itself never reaches RLS and is not a capability; `rls.apiKeys` reads a backend-set claim (`api_key`: `scopes`, `tenant`, `roles`) through `permdock_api_key_allows`, a ceiling and never a grant beyond the key's roles ([API keys](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/credentials.mdx)). `rls.tokenScopes` narrows a delegated token (`client_id`, or an `act` that is not a support or impersonation session) by its `scope` claim through `permdock_token_scope_allows`, over the map better-supabase reads as `delegation.scopes`.
- better-supabase: `permdock/better-supabase` fills better-supabase's runtime slots and names them after the slot, not the brand: `authorizationProvider` (its `authorization` config), `authorizationHooks` and `AuthorizationHooksOptions` (its `AuthorizationHooks`, the `authorization` option of `createServer`), `authorizer` (its `authorizer` server option, an `Authorizer` over `Permission` references that reads `subject.properties.claims`), `bucketPolicy` and `topicPolicy` (its storage and realtime access policies, taking `Permission` references and either `{ manifest, catalog }` or `{ policy, schema? }`), `apiKeyVerifier` (a `CredentialVerifier` over its API keys block), `apiKeyClaimOptions` (its `api_key` claim settings from `rls.apiKeys`), `toolPolicy` (its `createMcp` `authorize` and `visible` hooks), `credentialGuard` (a `CredentialProvider` that PermDock guards), `accessReviewApplier` (applies revoked access-review items through its organizations and invitations blocks), `subjectFromBetterSupabase` and `subjectFromShareLink` (a link subject from its share-links block's `resolve`, keyed by `subjectType` in `links`). `authorizationProvider`'s `approvers` option is keyed by better-supabase's approval kind and fills its `isApprover` function; `approver` becomes the `'ai-tool'` entry, and the provider never sets `canApprove`. `apiKeyVerifier` shares its name with the one in `permdock/server` on purpose: both fill the same `CredentialVerifier` slot over a different key store. `permdock/supabase` stays plain Supabase and never names better-supabase ([better-supabase](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/better-supabase.mdx)).
- Field security: RLS is row-level unless `rls generate --fields views` (`rls.fields: 'views'`) emits `<table>_visible`, a `security_invoker` view whose restricted columns are `case when <permitted> then col end` over the row policy's helper calls; `--revoke-columns` (`rls.revokeColumns`) keeps only unrestricted columns and the key readable on the table and reads the rest through the owner-rights `<table>_visible_fields` companion (joined on `permdock_key`, marked `permdock:field-companion`); `--fields mask` (`rls.fields: 'mask'`) adds `<schema>.<table>_mask(p_row)`, a `security invoker` function that nulls the restricted columns of one row by the same masks, for owner-rights SQL. Never Postgres column privileges per app role and never a permission per field. `rls import` returns `fieldViews`, `testRlsParity({ fieldViews })` compares with `pick`, doctor PD030 ([field security](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/rls.mdx)).
- Graph: a resource's `parent` may name itself, `restricted` names a boolean column or `{ field, stops }` (`'parent'` and link names it closes; a closed link also checks the rows above through `permdock_restricted_<resource>`), and relations are `{ field, memberOf? }`, `{ edge, object?, subject?, expiresAt? }` or `{ principal, period? }`. `relation(resource, name, { through: 'parent', depth })` walks the chain (default 16, at most 32) through a `RelationSource` (`ancestors`, `related`; `memoryRelationSource`, `testRelationSource`) passed as `relations`, cached per instance; `prefetchRelations` fills the cache and `whoCan` lists holders with `complete`, never granting. Reasons `relation-depth` and `relation-unavailable`. Snapshots carry no graph (graph grants are `portable: false`). RLS: `permdock_closure` with statement triggers, `permitted_<resource>_ids(relation)` (resource in snake_case), `rls.rowHelpers` with `permitted_<resource>_rows(permission)`, `_rows_for(p_user, permission, p_claims)` and the row-valued `permitted_<resource>_row(p_row, permission)`, `inherit(permission, { through })` as a grantee read through `RelationSource.row`, `rls verify --tree`, doctor PD031 / PD032 ([relationships](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/relationships.mdx)).
- Options, not exports: an option is named after its interface noun, and `store` means only `ApprovalStore`. The list: `webBotAuth`, `store` (`ApprovalStore`), `sink` (`DecisionSink`), `source` (`SnapshotSource`, on `permdock/react-native`), `policies` (`PolicySource`), `verifier` (`TokenVerifier` or `CredentialVerifier`), `signer` (`TokenSigner`), `memberships` (one `MembershipSource` or an array, composed), `customRoles` (`CustomRoleSource`), `entitlements` (`EntitlementSource`), `settings` (`SettingsSource`), `relations` (`RelationSource`), `limits`, `revocations` (`RevocationFeed`), `approvalPolicies` (`ApprovalPolicySource`), `approvalDelegations` (`ApprovalDelegationSource`), `principals` (`PrincipalSource`), `automations` (`AutomationSource`), `waiter` (`ApprovalWaiter`), `directory` (`DirectoryStore`, on `scimHandler`) and `reviews` (`AccessReviewStore`). The decision call site is `call`, never `source`. Snapshot persistence on `permdock/react-native` is a `SnapshotStorage`.
- Defaults, runners, sources, verifiers: every in-process default is `memory<Interface>` (`memorySettingsSource`, `memoryRelationSource`, `memoryDecisionSink`, `memoryApprovalPolicySource`, `memoryApprovalDelegationSource`, `memoryAccessReviewStore`, `memoryCredentialStore`, `memorySnapshotStorage`, `memoryPrincipalSource`). Every interface has a `test<Interface>` runner in `permdock/testing` (`testDecisionProvider`, `testSnapshotStorage`, `testPrincipalSource`), and a parity runner is `test*Parity` (`testRlsParity`, `testOrmParity`, `testClientParity`). A fixture helper names what it does: `snapshotFixture`, `seedRows(query, rows)`. An adapted source is `<origin><Interface>` (`stripeEntitlementSource`, `tableMembershipSource`, `junctionMembershipSource`, `tableApprovalDelegationSource`, `powerSyncSource`, plus `supabasePostgresQuery`); `fromSnapshot` keeps its name. A verifier factory is `<thing>Verifier(options)` (`webBotAuthVerifier`, `apiKeyVerifier`). Never a new `from*` source, a `verify*` factory, or a `memory*` name that is not an interface name.
- Hooks: one meaning per name. `onDenied` throws (core and `permdock/next`); `deniedResponse` builds the Problem Details response (server adapters); `deniedText` is the text a model reads (agent adapters); `data` loads the row, as on an operation binding. Never `loadData` or `load`.
- Context and prop types are adapter-first: `ExpressPermDockRequest`, `FastifyPermDockRequest`, `HonoPermDockEnv`, `ElysiaPermDockContext`, `ConvexPermDockContext`, `NextPermDockProviderProps`, `ReactNativePermDockProviderProps`, `SveltePermDockOptions`. Spell out `Context`, never `Ctx`.
- Brand casing: an acronym brand is one capital then lower case (`A2a`, `Ssf`, `Authzen`: `testAuthzen`, `AuthzenVectors`), and a camel-case brand keeps its capitals (`PowerSync`: `powerSyncSource`). Identifiers use the `-ize` spelling (`summarizeSubject`); prose stays British.
- Agent options: every agent adapter (`ai-sdk`, `openai`, `claude-agent`, `eve`) takes the full set `subject`, `actor`, `delegation`, `tenant`, `operations`, `models`, `store`, `unmapped`, `context`, `wrap`, `otel` and `deniedText`. `permdock/mcp` takes `actor` and `delegation` next to `actorKind`. `stepUp` means "send the user to raise their own access" on `permdock/mcp` (`{ at }`) and `permdock/ai-sdk` (`{ at? }`, a denial a sign-in or an eligible activation lifts becomes `user-approval` with a `StepUp` hint); never `withStepUp` or a wrapper around `toolApproval`.
- Failure causes: a failure-cause type is `<Thing>FailureCause` and its field is `cause` (`WebhookFailureCause`, `ApprovalInspectResult.cause`); `reason` is kept for the closed denial list. Error codes have one spelling each, such as `approval-not-pending`.
- Definitions and policy: `definePermissions`, `resource`, `definePolicy`, `role`, `allow`, `deny` and the other plain functions on `concepts/policies.mdx`; `crud` / `readable` / `writable` are option factories for `resource()`. Never a fluent policy builder.
- Errors: `PermDockDeniedError`, `PermDockApprovalRequiredError`, `PermDockValidationError` and `PermDockRevokedError`. Any other error class is a domain error (`ApprovalError`, `AccessReviewError`, `DirectoryUniquenessError`, `DirectoryNotFoundError`, `InvalidSignatureError`), listed on `getting-started/naming.mdx`, with an `is<Name>` guard (`isApprovalError`, `isAccessReviewError`). Web Bot Auth failures throw `InvalidSignatureError` and never become an anonymous actor.
- Open and closed unions. An id that core only compares for equality, where an unknown value can only fail to match, is an `OpenUnion` (`T | (string & {})`): `ActorKind`, `AdapterId`, `MembershipVia`, `DirectoryEvent.source`. A union whose members core interprets one by one stays closed: denial reasons, trace skip reasons, approval modes and statuses, condition operators, credential kinds, operation risks, delivery channels, problem types and preset ids.
- Denial reasons are a closed list (`concepts/decisions.mdx`). A boundary failure is `validation`, never `invalid-resource`; an undeclared role is `unknown-role`.
- Never `SnapshotV2` (the major is the `v` field), never `@permdock/cloud` (it is the `permdock/cloud` entry), never an entry for a tool PermDock only composes with, such as `permdock/hey-api`, `permdock/scalar` or `permdock/mintlify`. An entry is core, a feature contract (`permdock/agent`, `permdock/workflow`, `permdock/delivery`, `permdock/chat`), an implementation named after the npm package whose hooks it maps onto a contract (`permdock/ai-sdk`, `permdock/resend`), or `permdock/cloud` ([adapters](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/index.mdx)). `with*` belongs to the Supabase middleware pipeline, to `withSubject`, the transaction helper of `permdock/drizzle`, `permdock/kysely` and `permdock/prisma`, and to `withOtel`, the instance wrapper of `permdock/otel`. `permdock/terminal` is for consumers' CLIs and is not the `permdock` binary or `permdock/cli`; never a scoped `@permdock/*` package (`permdock`, owned by the `scaledockhq` npm organisation, is the only published name); the build hooks `createPermDockPlugin` / `createPermDockUnplugin` collect only.
- OpenAPI output: only `x-permdock-*`, registered `x-oai-*` names, and the opt-in `x-badges` hint. Never another party's namespace (`x-scalar-*`, `x-speakeasy-*`, `x-fern-*`, `x-gnap`, gateway namespaces such as `x-amazon-apigateway-*`) and never `targetFormat`. Draft constructs (OpenAPI 3.3 profiles, Overlay 1.2) always ship next to a stable twin with the pin in `x-permdock-catalog.drafts`.
- A field a specification already names keeps the spec's name; add a row to the "Spec names" table on `getting-started/naming.mdx` before adding it.
- Approvals refuse the requester: `distinct` defaults to `true`, `distinct: false` is the per-grant opt-out (doctor PD024), and `requireDistinctApprover` is a handler-wide floor over it. Never an `allowSelf` flag. An out-of-office cover is an `ApprovalDelegation` (`delegator`, `delegate`, `from`, `until`) read from an `ApprovalDelegationSource` (`approvalDelegations`, `memoryApprovalDelegationSource`, `tableApprovalDelegationSource` over the `approval_delegations` table `rls.approvals.delegations` writes); the signature records `by` as the delegate and `onBehalfOf` plus `delegation` for the person they covered. One hop. With better-supabase installed, PermDock owns covers: `permdock_is_approver`'s four-argument form reads the same table. A link or a feed cannot approve.
- Approvers: `user(id)` for one person, `relation()` checked only through verdict facts from `approverRelations`, `holder(permission)` checked only through `verdict.permissions` from `approverPermissions`, `anyOf(...)` for any-of groups (a list or `allOf(...)` is all-of), `mode` `'any' | 'all' | 'sequential'` with `stages`. Data rules are an `ApprovalPolicySource` (`approvalPolicies`), which only adds stages and fails closed. Never `steps`, `chain` or `approvalRules`. Access reviews use the same approver forms as `reviewers` on an `AccessReview` (`AccessReviewStore` as `reviews`, `memoryAccessReviewStore`, `startAccessReview`, `accessReviewsHandler`); a reviewer cannot decide their own holding.
- Renames: `definePermissions(tree, { renamed })` maps former keys to current ones; the catalog field is `renamedFrom`, the diff kinds are `renamed` and `alias-removed`. Never `aliases` or `legacyKeys`.
- CLI flags share better-supabase's vocabulary (`.agents/rules/cli.mdc`): `--out <file>` and `--out-dir <dir>`, `--format` for the renderer (`text`, `json`, `github`, `sarif`) and `--kind` for the document, `--only` and `--ignore` to narrow, `--force` only to overwrite, `--fail-on <severity>`, `--check` and `--dry-run`, `--confirm <token>`, `--db-url-stdin` (then `DATABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_DB_URL`), `--policy` and `--permissions` for modules, `--for` for a skills target. Problem `code` values are snake_case (`unknown_flag`, `consent_required`). Never `--strict`, `--write`, `--db`, `--from` for a module, `--format` for a document kind, `--client` for a skills target, or a secret as a flag value: secrets come from a `--<name>-stdin` flag or the environment.
- Renamed identifiers: a renamed public identifier gets a row in `packages/permdock/src/cli/codemod/renames-<version>.ts` (`permdock codemod <version>`), the same `| Old | New |` row in its changeset, and a row in the "Renamed in <version>" section of `getting-started/naming.mdx`; `pnpm docs:drift` checks that the three agree and that no docs page uses an old name.
- Approvals resume one call: `ApprovalStore.consume` sets `consumedAt`. The HTTP resume header is `PermDock-Approval` ([approvals](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/approvals.mdx)).
- Snapshots: `snapshotFor` and `mayAccess` are the cache building blocks. PermDock never adds `'use cache'` or calls `headers()` / `cookies()` itself; `getSnapshot` on the `permdock/next` factory calls `cacheLife` and `cacheTag` only inside the app's own `'use cache: private'` scope; `tenants: 'all'` is opt-in ([Next.js Cache Components](../../apps/docs/content/docs/guides/next-cache-components.mdx)).
- Custom roles: `scope: 'global'` marks a platform custom role (`CustomRoleSource.globalRoles`), capped by assignable global roles; `shared: true` marks a shared custom role (`CustomRoleSource.sharedRoles`, `SharedCustomRole`), held in every tenant, whose name wins over a tenant role's (`name-taken`). `CustomRole.grants` entries are `{ permission, effect?, level? }` and carry no condition, approval or limit; `level` names a condition the resource declares in code (`resource(…, { levels })`), and an undeclared one is `unknown-level` and denies; `orderedLevels: true` on the resource lets a held level hand out every level declared before it. `resolveCustomRole` is the only resolver (evaluation, `snapshot`, `snapshotFor`, `validateCustomRole`, doctor PD023); the ceiling is the code allows of declared `assignable` roles in the role's scope, never hosted grants. `assignablePermissions` / `useAssignablePermissions` sit next to `assignableRoles`; `meta.manageRoles` on a role or permission lifts the held-grant intersection ([custom roles](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/custom-roles.mdx)).
- RLS custom-role and seed names: `rls.customRoles.from.sync: 'trigger'` (`permdock_sync_custom_role_*`, with the before-row `permdock_sync_custom_role_guard_*` caller checks), `permdock_custom_role_guard(p_tenant, p_scope, p_scope_id, p_role)` and `permdock_custom_role_beyond(p_tenant, p_scope, p_scope_id, p_allow, p_deny, p_include)` as the stable checks for an app's own write path, `permdock_custom_role_name_guard` on `rls.customRoleWrites.roles`, `rls.customRoleWrites.roles.primaryKey` with `permdock_replace_custom_role_grants_by_id`, `permdock_trusted_replace_custom_role_grants_by_id` and `permdock_trusted_role_permissions_by_id`, `rls.customRoleWrites.audit` with the `permdock.custom_role.replaced` event, `permdock_custom_role_entry_problem(p_scope, p_entry)` with `parseCustomRoleEntry(policy, entry, { scope })` from `permdock`, the `RoleMap` role column `{ column, map }`, `rls.seeds: 'function'` with `<schema>.seed()`, `rls.seeds: 'view'`, `rls.tables.<resource>.via` with `permitted_<parent>_ids`, `permdock rls explain`, `supabase.hook.teams` with the `team_ids` claim, and doctor `PD072` and `PD073`. A seeded grant key is `<permission>[#<n>|#<group>][@<level>]` and a custom role entry `<permission>[@<level>]` ([RLS](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/rls.mdx)).
- Ownership: role options `min`, `max`, `transferOnly`, `assigns`, `for` and `meta.audience` (`RoleMeta`); `permdock.decideRoleChange(change)` (`scope: 'global'` with no `id` for a role with no `on`; `assignableRoles({ scope: 'global' })` lists them) answers `granted` / `denied` with the closed reasons `last-holder`, `max-holders`, `transfer-only`, `not-assignable-by`, `self-demotion`, `not-allowed-for-membership`, `conflicting-role`, takes the actor from the instance and fails closed without `holders`; a role held through a kind outside `for` is dropped at resolution; an `assigns` graph replaces the held-grant rule for `assignableRoles` and ranks `heldRoles`; `audiences()` / `snapshot.audiences` never grant. SQL objects are `permdock_can_assign`, `permdock_holders_<scope>`, `permdock_transfer_only_<scope>_*` and the `rls.assignments` triggers `permdock_assignment_<table>` on the membership, global-roles and listed tables, with `ownRole: 'refuse'` for the caller's own rows, and `permdock_replace_global_roles(p_user, p_roles)` (invoker rights) over the `rls.roles` table; doctor PD026 and PD064 ([ownership](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/ownership.mdx)).
- Streams and sockets: `connection(...)` returns a frozen `Connection`. `RevocationFeed` (`memoryRevocationFeed()`) ends or revalidates a connection and never grants. `PermDockRevokedError` is only a connection signal. An abort adds no denial reason ([streams](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/streams.mdx)).

